7.6, "baseline_margin": -18.26, "analyst_adjustment": -1.74, "key_factors": ["R+15 partisan lean in a district Republicans have held for decades", "Overwhelming money gap: Downing raised $2.1M with $650K on hand vs. Miller's $19K", "September Eagleton poll shows Downing leading 48-26; Polymarket prices a Miller win at ~4%", "Downing's 34% approval is soft but unlikely to matter at this lean"], "rationale": "Every piece of evidence points the same way: a fifteen-point Republican lean, a roughly hundred-to-one fundraising advantage for the incumbent, a September poll showing a 22-point Downing lead, and markets giving Miller about a four percent chance. Downing's modest 34% approval is the only soft spot, but nothing suggests a genuinely competitive race.", "watch": "A sharp late national wave toward Democrats or an unexpected scandal could tighten the race, though the money and polling gaps leave very little room for an upset.", "confidence": "high", "notes": ["No expert rater lists this race; treated as safe for the party holding the seat.", "Incumbent Troy Downing (R) is running."]}, "overview": "Montana's second district is a deeply Republican seat, with a partisan lean of about fifteen points toward the GOP, and no Democratic nominee has carried it in decades. First-term Representative Troy Downing enters the general election as a heavy favorite, backed by roughly $2.1 million raised and $650,000 on hand, while Democratic nominee Brian Miller has raised about $19,000. The September Eagleton poll put Downing ahead 48 to 26 among registered voters, and prediction markets give Miller roughly a four percent chance.\n\nDowning's first-term record has centered on Montana water-rights and rural infrastructure bills that advanced through Congress, and those measures have dominated local coverage of the race. Miller's bid focuses on ending a half-century without Democratic representation in the district but lacks the resources to compete. An independent candidate and a Libertarian are also on the ballot but have not registered as significant factors.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.0084, "margin": -18.82}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.0066, "margin": -20.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.0073, "margin": -20.0}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.0079, "margin": -20.0}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.0127, "margin": -18.0}, {"date": "2026-09-27", "p_dem": 0.007, "margin": -20.0}, {"date": "2026-09-28", "p_dem": 0.0099, "margin": -19.0}, {"date": "2026-09-29", "p_dem": 0.0089, "margin": -20.0}, {"date": "2026-09-30", "p_dem": 0.0077, "margin": -20.0}, {"date": "2026-10-01", "p_dem": 0.0107, "margin": -19.0}, {"date": "2026-10-02", "p_dem": 0.0053, "margin": -20.0}, {"date": "2026-10-03", "p_dem": 0.0119, "margin": -18.0}, {"date": "2026-10-04", "p_dem": 0.0108, "margin": -20.0}]}